1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,525
A release script at your company builds a
commit without a working tree, the folder

2
00:00:09,565 --> 00:00:14,406
of files you normally edit. It writes the
objects, it prints a commit ID, and it

3
00:00:14,446 --> 00:00:19,471
exits with status zero, the usual sign of
success. Then the next step runs git log,

4
00:00:19,511 --> 00:00:22,705
and Git says the branch
doesn't have any commits yet.

5
00:00:23,045 --> 00:00:27,922
The commit exists. You can print it
by its ID. And no history shows it.

6
00:00:27,962 --> 00:00:29,376
How can both be true?

7
00:00:31,716 --> 00:00:35,772
To explain that, you have to stop
thinking of git commit as one act.

8
00:00:35,812 --> 00:00:40,832
It's five moves. When a Git command surprises
you, the useful question is always the

9
00:00:40,872 --> 00:00:44,507
same: which objects did it write,
and which refs did it move?

10
00:00:44,547 --> 00:00:49,266
A ref is a name that points at an object.
Today you make a commit with your own

11
00:00:49,306 --> 00:00:53,904
hands, one move at a time, and you see
exactly which move was missing in that

12
00:00:53,944 --> 00:00:54,326
script.

13
00:00:56,166 --> 00:00:59,913
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:00:59,953 --> 00:01:04,321
In the last video you saw three kinds
of object: blob, tree and commit.

15
00:01:04,361 --> 00:01:09,226
A blob is the content of one file, a tree
is one directory, and a commit is one

16
00:01:09,266 --> 00:01:14,379
saved snapshot. There's a fourth, the tag
object, and this video opens one of each.

17
00:01:14,719 --> 00:01:19,486
Then we put the porcelain away. Porcelain
is Git's word for the everyday commands,

18
00:01:19,526 --> 00:01:24,411
such as git add and git commit. Plumbing is
the low-level layer underneath, and five

19
00:01:24,451 --> 00:01:28,045
plumbing commands do the same
work in separate, visible steps.

20
00:01:28,085 --> 00:01:32,853
We'll build a commit from nothing: no file
in the working tree, no git add, no git

21
00:01:32,893 --> 00:01:37,367
commit. After each step I'll ask you
which object or file is about to appear.

22
00:01:37,407 --> 00:01:40,415
And keep that release script
in mind. It comes back.

23
00:01:42,255 --> 00:01:43,834
Here's what you'll walk away with.

24
00:01:44,174 --> 00:01:45,586
After this video you can:

25
00:01:45,926 --> 00:01:50,440
Name the four object types, and say what
each contains and what it points at.

26
00:01:50,780 --> 00:01:54,795
Read a tree entry: mode,
type, object ID, name.

27
00:01:55,135 --> 00:01:58,256
Build a commit with plumbing only,
without git add or git commit.

28
00:01:58,596 --> 00:02:02,725
Explain which step of the hand-built commit
corresponds to which porcelain command.

29
00:02:04,565 --> 00:02:05,855
The four object types.

30
00:02:06,196 --> 00:02:09,965
A blob holds the bytes of one
file. It refers to nothing.

31
00:02:10,005 --> 00:02:11,636
It is written by git add.

32
00:02:11,976 --> 00:02:16,887
A tree holds one directory: for each
entry, a mode, a name and an object ID.

33
00:02:16,927 --> 00:02:22,296
It refers to blobs and to other trees. It
is written by git commit, from the index.

34
00:02:22,636 --> 00:02:27,455
A commit holds one tree ID, the parent
commit IDs, an author, a committer and a

35
00:02:27,495 --> 00:02:32,376
message. It refers to its tree and its
parents. It is written by git commit, git

36
00:02:32,416 --> 00:02:34,467
merge, git cherry-pick and others.

37
00:02:34,807 --> 00:02:40,743
A tag object holds the ID and type of another
object, a tag name, the tagger, and a

38
00:02:40,783 --> 00:02:45,783
message. It refers, usually, to a
commit. It is written by git tag -a.

39
00:02:46,123 --> 00:02:49,255
Names live in trees. A blob
is content without a name.

40
00:02:49,295 --> 00:02:52,485
The name of a file is stored
in the tree that lists it.

41
00:02:52,525 --> 00:02:57,183
On screen is the scorer from the last
video: the tree holds the three names, and

42
00:02:57,223 --> 00:03:02,116
each name points at a blob. This is why, in
the last video, two files with different

43
00:03:02,156 --> 00:03:04,407
names and the same bytes were one blob.

44
00:03:04,747 --> 00:03:09,360
Reading a tree entry. Four fields:
mode, type, object ID, name.

45
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:12,092
The mode tells you the kind of entry.

46
00:03:12,432 --> 00:03:16,745
100644 is a regular file.
100755 is an executable file.

47
00:03:16,785 --> 00:03:22,839
120000 is a symbolic link. 040000 is a
directory: the entry points at a tree.

48
00:03:22,879 --> 00:03:29,487
160000 is a gitlink: a commit of another
repository, which you met in video 6 as the

49
00:03:29,527 --> 00:03:35,740
embedded repository. Executable or not
is the only permission that Git records.

50
00:03:35,780 --> 00:03:39,539
It stores no owner and no
other permission bits.

51
00:03:39,879 --> 00:03:45,000
The tag object, and the tag without one.
An annotated tag is a ref that points at a

52
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:50,099
tag object, and the tag object names a
commit. A lightweight tag has no object: it

53
00:03:50,139 --> 00:03:54,763
is a ref that points at the commit
directly. A later chapter develops tags.

54
00:03:55,103 --> 00:03:56,439
A commit in five moves.

55
00:03:56,779 --> 00:04:00,416
Step 1: git hash-object -w
stores content as a blob.

56
00:04:00,456 --> 00:04:04,235
🟢 SAFE. The porcelain that
does it for you is git add.

57
00:04:04,575 --> 00:04:10,354
Step 2: git update-index --add --cacheinfo,
with a mode, an ID and a path, records

58
00:04:10,394 --> 00:04:16,316
in the index that this path has this content.
🟡 CAUTION: it changes one index entry.

59
00:04:16,356 --> 00:04:20,290
The porcelain is again git
add. So git add is two moves.

60
00:04:20,630 --> 00:04:24,569
Step 3: git write-tree writes
the index as tree objects.

61
00:04:24,609 --> 00:04:27,128
🟢 SAFE. The porcelain is git commit.

62
00:04:27,468 --> 00:04:32,967
Step 4: git commit-tree, with a tree,
optionally -p and a parent, and -m with a

63
00:04:33,007 --> 00:04:37,805
message, writes a commit object. 🟢
SAFE. The porcelain is git commit.

64
00:04:38,145 --> 00:04:43,138
Step 5: git update-ref refs/heads/<branch>
<commit> points the branch at the commit.

65
00:04:43,178 --> 00:04:47,631
🟡 CAUTION: it moves one ref, and nothing
else. The porcelain is git commit.

66
00:04:47,671 --> 00:04:49,369
So git commit is three moves.

67
00:04:49,709 --> 00:04:55,168
Steps 1, 3 and 4 only add objects. No ref
and no index entry changes, and garbage

68
00:04:55,208 --> 00:05:00,327
collection removes objects that nothing
refers to. That's why they're green.

69
00:05:00,667 --> 00:05:04,485
What porcelain adds. git commit
does more than steps 3 to 5.

70
00:05:04,525 --> 00:05:07,827
It refuses to record nothing,
as you saw in video 4.

71
00:05:07,867 --> 00:05:12,713
It runs hooks, the scripts a repository
can run at moments such as a commit.

72
00:05:12,753 --> 00:05:18,049
And it writes a reason into the reflogs.
git update-ref writes reflog lines for the

73
00:05:18,089 --> 00:05:21,778
branch and for HEAD without a
message, unless you pass -m.

74
00:05:22,118 --> 00:05:25,154
When to use plumbing, and when
not. For daily work, don't.

75
00:05:25,194 --> 00:05:29,505
git add, git commit, git switch and git
branch make the same objects and refs, and

76
00:05:29,545 --> 00:05:33,802
add checks. The read-only plumbing, git
cat-file, git ls-tree, git rev-parse, git

77
00:05:33,842 --> 00:05:38,205
for-each-ref and git merge-base, is always
appropriate, and settles arguments about

78
00:05:38,245 --> 00:05:38,948
state fastest.

79
00:05:39,288 --> 00:05:43,684
And know the limits of the writing
plumbing: git update-ref verifies little.

80
00:05:43,724 --> 00:05:48,179
It refuses a blob as the value of a
branch and a stale expected value, but it

81
00:05:48,219 --> 00:05:52,908
accepts any existing commit, and it never
looks at the index or the working tree.

82
00:05:53,248 --> 00:05:58,347
Recovery. The plumbing is worth knowing
because recovery uses the same tools.

83
00:05:58,387 --> 00:06:02,951
A commit found by git fsck or in a
reflog is reattached with one ref.

84
00:06:04,791 --> 00:06:07,952
Think of the objects as
documents in a filing system.

85
00:06:07,992 --> 00:06:12,783
A blob is a page with no title. A tree is
a table of contents: titles, and where

86
00:06:12,823 --> 00:06:17,614
each page or sub-table is filed. A commit
is a cover sheet: "the project is this

87
00:06:17,654 --> 00:06:22,505
table of contents; it follows that earlier
cover sheet; signed by this author and

88
00:06:22,545 --> 00:06:27,397
this committer, with this note". A tag
object is a labelled note that points at a

89
00:06:27,437 --> 00:06:28,121
cover sheet.

90
00:06:28,461 --> 00:06:33,271
The model breaks at one point that matters:
in a filing system you could pull a page

91
00:06:33,311 --> 00:06:38,122
and correct it. Here you can't. Every one
of these documents is filed under a number

92
00:06:38,162 --> 00:06:42,798
computed from its content. So a corrected
page is a second, different page with a

93
00:06:42,838 --> 00:06:47,649
different number, and every table of contents
and cover sheet that should include it

94
00:06:47,689 --> 00:06:48,861
must be written anew.

95
00:06:49,201 --> 00:06:53,432
For the five moves: git add is "file the
page and enter it in the draft table of

96
00:06:53,472 --> 00:06:57,862
contents". git commit is "write out the
tables, write the cover sheet, and move the

97
00:06:57,902 --> 00:06:58,396
bookmark".

98
00:07:00,236 --> 00:07:03,882
All eight objects, and the
two names that lead to them.

99
00:07:03,922 --> 00:07:08,438
The branch main points at the commit.
The commit points at one tree.

100
00:07:08,478 --> 00:07:13,263
The tree has three entries: a blob
and two more trees, each with a blob.

101
00:07:13,303 --> 00:07:18,825
And from the top left, the ref v0.1.0 points
at the tag object, which points at the

102
00:07:18,865 --> 00:07:19,629
same commit.

103
00:07:19,969 --> 00:07:24,501
The five moves as a ladder: the plumbing
command, what appears, and the porcelain

104
00:07:24,541 --> 00:07:25,687
that does it for you.

105
00:07:27,527 --> 00:07:31,516
A repository called ranker, with
one commit and one annotated tag.

106
00:07:31,556 --> 00:07:32,737
First the inventory.

107
00:07:33,077 --> 00:07:37,878
Eight objects: three blobs, three
trees, one commit, one tag object.

108
00:07:37,918 --> 00:07:39,302
We open one of each.

109
00:07:39,642 --> 00:07:44,430
A commit. This one has no parent
line, so it's a root commit.

110
00:07:44,470 --> 00:07:50,524
The number after the email address,
followed by +0530, is a time in Git's raw

111
00:07:50,564 --> 00:07:56,698
format: seconds since the first of January
1970 UTC, then the offset from UTC.

112
00:07:57,038 --> 00:08:03,852
Trees. git cat-file -p prints one level
of a tree. git ls-tree -r walks down and

113
00:08:03,892 --> 00:08:10,878
prints every file with its full path. Read
one entry in four fields: 100755, blob,

114
00:08:10,918 --> 00:08:17,304
the ID beginning aa1a031, scripts/test.sh.
Mode 100755: an executable file.

115
00:08:17,644 --> 00:08:22,893
Try it now. Thirty seconds: find the
README.md line and read it out loud in four

116
00:08:22,933 --> 00:08:24,942
fields. Then check your answer.

117
00:08:29,782 --> 00:08:36,670
Mode 100644, a regular file. Type
blob. The ID beginning 895d61e.

118
00:08:36,710 --> 00:08:39,228
And the name, README.md.

119
00:08:39,568 --> 00:08:45,241
A blob: two lines of Python and nothing
else. The name rerank.py isn't in it.

120
00:08:45,281 --> 00:08:47,616
It's stored in the tree 95722c3.

121
00:08:47,956 --> 00:08:54,442
And the tag object, 0f7f193. It names the
commit e375b7b and its type, repeats the

122
00:08:54,482 --> 00:08:57,785
tag name, and adds a tagger and a message.

123
00:08:58,125 --> 00:09:02,727
A repository with no working-tree file at
all. The content arrives through a pipe.

124
00:09:03,067 --> 00:09:09,591
Step 1. git hash-object -w 🟢 SAFE. Predict:
after this command, what exists under

125
00:09:09,631 --> 00:09:14,047
.git/objects, and does an
index exist? Say it out loud.

126
00:09:14,087 --> 00:09:14,857
I'll wait.

127
00:09:19,697 --> 00:09:24,696
One object exists, the blob
a7f6bbc. There is no index yet.

128
00:09:25,036 --> 00:09:28,515
Step 2. git update-index
--add --cacheinfo 🟡 CAUTION.

129
00:09:28,555 --> 00:09:32,101
--cacheinfo takes a mode, a
full object ID and a path.

130
00:09:32,141 --> 00:09:35,686
--add permits a path that
the index does not have yet.

131
00:09:35,726 --> 00:09:41,064
Predict what git status will say about a
path that is in the index, in no commit,

132
00:09:41,104 --> 00:09:42,126
and not on disk.

133
00:09:46,966 --> 00:09:50,453
git status reports the path twice,
and both lines are correct.

134
00:09:50,493 --> 00:09:53,922
"new file" compares the index
with HEAD, which has no commit.

135
00:09:53,962 --> 00:09:58,700
"deleted" compares the working tree with
the index, which lists a file that isn't on

136
00:09:58,740 --> 00:10:03,080
disk. Two comparisons, two answers. If
that looked like a bug, you're in good

137
00:10:03,120 --> 00:10:05,582
company. Remember this
picture for video 10.

138
00:10:05,922 --> 00:10:11,186
Step 3. git write-tree 🟢 SAFE. The index
holds one path, prompts/system.txt.

139
00:10:11,226 --> 00:10:15,721
Quick quiz: how many tree objects
will it write? Option one: one.

140
00:10:15,761 --> 00:10:18,024
Option two: two. Say it out loud.

141
00:10:22,864 --> 00:10:28,827
Two. The index is a flat list of full
paths, and trees are nested, one object per

142
00:10:28,867 --> 00:10:34,756
directory. So git write-tree wrote 5445e77
for the top directory and 4015b7b for

143
00:10:34,796 --> 00:10:35,424
prompts/.

144
00:10:35,764 --> 00:10:40,999
Step 4. git commit-tree 🟢 SAFE. Predict:
after this, will git log show the commit?

145
00:10:45,839 --> 00:10:51,176
git commit-tree wrote the commit cc0ef15,
with the author and the committer taken

146
00:10:51,216 --> 00:10:55,159
from the environment, as git
commit does. And git log fails.

147
00:10:55,199 --> 00:10:59,275
HEAD names the branch main, and
no ref refs/heads/main exists.

148
00:10:59,315 --> 00:11:04,652
git fsck, which checks the object database,
calls the commit dangling: it exists,

149
00:11:04,692 --> 00:11:06,312
and nothing refers to it.

150
00:11:06,652 --> 00:11:10,681
That's the release script from the
hook. It stopped after step 4.

151
00:11:10,721 --> 00:11:13,311
The commit existed, and no name led to it.

152
00:11:13,651 --> 00:11:18,114
Step 5. git update-ref 🟡 CAUTION,
with the full name refs/heads/main.

153
00:11:18,454 --> 00:11:22,946
One ref makes the difference: git
log works and git fsck is silent.

154
00:11:22,986 --> 00:11:28,629
And git status --short prints D prompts/system.txt:
the commit and the index contain

155
00:11:28,669 --> 00:11:34,312
the file, the working tree doesn't. The
commit exists, and the working tree is still

156
00:11:34,352 --> 00:11:34,718
empty.

157
00:11:35,058 --> 00:11:40,039
The last step fills the working tree, and
it uses a command with a red label, so the

158
00:11:40,079 --> 00:11:43,685
five questions come first. git
restore <path> is 🔴 DANGEROUS.

159
00:11:43,725 --> 00:11:48,168
What it changes: it overwrites the file
in the working tree from the index.

160
00:11:48,208 --> 00:11:51,276
What it can destroy:
uncommitted edits in that file.

161
00:11:51,616 --> 00:11:55,979
How to preview: git diff -- <path>
shows what would be overwritten.

162
00:11:56,019 --> 00:12:00,644
How to recover: there is no recovery
for content that was never staged.

163
00:12:00,684 --> 00:12:05,770
When it's appropriate: when you've looked
at that diff and want to discard it.

164
00:12:05,810 --> 00:12:09,844
Twig looks worried, and with a
red label on screen, fairly so.

165
00:12:09,884 --> 00:12:13,656
Here, though, nothing can be
lost, because no file exists.

166
00:12:13,996 --> 00:12:16,963
The file is on disk, and
the working tree is clean.

167
00:12:17,303 --> 00:12:21,852
The state table of the five moves.
git hash-object -w: one new blob.

168
00:12:21,892 --> 00:12:26,847
git update-index: one index entry. git
write-tree: one tree per directory.

169
00:12:27,187 --> 00:12:31,204
git commit-tree: one commit
object, no ref, no reflog line.

170
00:12:31,544 --> 00:12:35,433
git update-ref: the branch ref.
Everything else is unchanged.

171
00:12:37,273 --> 00:12:38,921
Five mistakes to watch for.

172
00:12:39,261 --> 00:12:42,480
A commit made with git
commit-tree is not in git log.

173
00:12:42,520 --> 00:12:47,400
Root cause: the commit object exists and
no ref points at it; git fsck reports a

174
00:12:47,440 --> 00:12:51,274
dangling commit, and one git
update-ref or git branch fixes it.

175
00:12:51,614 --> 00:12:56,678
Looking for the file name inside the blob.
Root cause: a blob is content without a

176
00:12:56,718 --> 00:12:59,479
name; names and modes
are stored in the tree.

177
00:12:59,819 --> 00:13:04,648
After git update-ref moved the current
branch, git status shows changes that nobody

178
00:13:04,688 --> 00:13:09,340
made. Root cause: the ref moved and the
index and working tree did not; move the

179
00:13:09,380 --> 00:13:12,566
current branch with porcelain,
which updates all three.

180
00:13:12,906 --> 00:13:17,578
Giving git update-ref a short name. Root
cause: plumbing takes the name literally;

181
00:13:17,618 --> 00:13:21,888
write the full name beginning with
refs/. The next video shows what happens

182
00:13:21,928 --> 00:13:22,462
otherwise.

183
00:13:22,802 --> 00:13:27,672
Assuming Git records file permissions and
owners. Root cause: executable or not is

184
00:13:27,712 --> 00:13:29,888
the only permission that Git records.

185
00:13:31,728 --> 00:13:36,286
Now, out of the lab. A data-platform
team has a job that publishes generated

186
00:13:36,326 --> 00:13:39,614
configuration as commits, on
a server with no checkout.

187
00:13:39,654 --> 00:13:44,696
It uses exactly the moves you saw: store
the blobs, register them in an index, write

188
00:13:44,736 --> 00:13:49,476
the tree, write the commit with the
previous commit as its parent, and move the

189
00:13:49,516 --> 00:13:49,899
branch.

190
00:13:50,239 --> 00:13:55,004
The textbook's advice for such scripts is
in its table of what can go wrong: create

191
00:13:55,044 --> 00:13:59,577
the commit and move the ref together. A
script that creates the commit and then

192
00:13:59,617 --> 00:14:03,919
fails before moving the ref leaves a
dangling commit that no history shows.

193
00:14:04,259 --> 00:14:09,103
One more production point from the textbook:
author and committer are text supplied

194
00:14:09,143 --> 00:14:12,281
by whoever creates the commit.
Git doesn't check them.

195
00:14:12,321 --> 00:14:16,341
A signature makes authorship verifiable,
and later chapters cover it.

196
00:14:18,181 --> 00:14:25,141
Your turn. Do Lab 1.1, "Build a commit by
hand", in lab-manual/m01-what-git-is.md.

197
00:14:25,481 --> 00:14:30,317
Before each of the five steps, write down
which object or file will appear and what

198
00:14:30,357 --> 00:14:35,194
git status and git log will say afterwards.
The step where your prediction fails is

199
00:14:35,234 --> 00:14:36,310
the step to repeat.

200
00:14:38,150 --> 00:14:41,933
Q66: "What are a blob, a
tree and a commit object?"

201
00:14:44,273 --> 00:14:48,906
Answer out loud. The question sounds
elementary, and it's used to find out whether

202
00:14:48,946 --> 00:14:53,466
you know the model or only the words. A
strong answer says for each type what it

203
00:14:53,506 --> 00:14:57,512
contains, what it refers to and, as
important, what it doesn't contain.

204
00:14:57,552 --> 00:15:01,730
Then it connects them: how you get from
a commit to the bytes of one file.

205
00:15:01,770 --> 00:15:04,123
Offer the fourth type without being asked.

206
00:15:05,963 --> 00:15:10,823
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say: Git has four object types.

207
00:15:10,863 --> 00:15:16,319
A blob is file content with no name. A tree
is one directory: modes, names and IDs.

208
00:15:16,359 --> 00:15:21,087
A commit is one tree, its parents, an
author, a committer and a message.

209
00:15:21,127 --> 00:15:23,339
A tag object names another object.

210
00:15:23,679 --> 00:15:27,332
git add is two moves: store the
blob and record it in the index.

211
00:15:27,372 --> 00:15:31,314
git commit is three: write the trees,
write the commit, move the ref.

212
00:15:31,354 --> 00:15:35,642
A commit that no ref points at exists
and is dangling, and one ref makes it

213
00:15:35,682 --> 00:15:36,219
reachable.

214
00:15:38,059 --> 00:15:41,471
Read sections 2.5 and 2.6 of Chapter 2.

215
00:15:41,811 --> 00:15:46,582
Repeat Lab 1.1, "Build a commit by
hand", a second time from memory, with a

216
00:15:46,622 --> 00:15:48,635
different file name and message.

217
